1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Listen to part of a lecture in a United States history class.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:04,919
In the days before telephones,

3
00:00:04,919 --> 00:00:10,419
radio, and television, the only network of public communication that could reach farmers was the mail.

4
00:00:10,419 --> 00:00:14,200
But this wasn't the mail as we know it today.

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,280
At that time, in the early nineteenth century

6
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:20,012
mail delivery was uneven and widely scattered.

7
00:00:20,012 --> 00:00:25,050
In fact, many people living in rural areas got no mail at all.

8
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:27,349
In the early decades of the century,

9
00:00:27,349 --> 00:00:31,290
mail carriers were privately employed - they did not work for the government.

10
00:00:31,290 --> 00:00:35,471
Many years later, when the government finally took charge of delivering mail,

11
00:00:35,471 --> 00:00:40,000
it was mostly in cities that mail got delivered to people's homes.

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,793
So farmers still had a problem -

13
00:00:41,793 --> 00:00:44,760
they had to go to a post office to collect their mail

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,768
which, by the way, wasn't always nearby.

15
00:00:47,768 --> 00:00:52,250
Farmers' requests to have mail brought to their homes were at first met with outrage.

16
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:55,787
What could be more ridiculous, many urban residents asked,

17
00:00:55,787 --> 00:01:01,290
than paying government employees to travel miles across the countryside with an occasional letter?

18
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:09,190
Nevertheless, farmers' organizations succeeded in convincing the United States Congress that farmers needed mail delivery.

19
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:14,736
Finally in 1891 rural free delivery, known as RFD, came into being.

20
00:01:14,736 --> 00:01:22,130
In a sense, rural free delivery was the most important communications revolution in United States history.

21
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:28,050
Rural Americans were not lifted out of the relatively isolated communities they lived in.

22
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:29,740
Because of rural free delivery

23
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:34,040
there now ran a highway to the world from every farmer's doorstep.
